Your browser blocks the response because `http://localhost:5173` and `http://localhost:8080` are different origins, and your API does not send a header that allows the page at 5173 to read its responses. An origin is the scheme, host, and port of a URL together. If the port is different, the origin is different.

CORS (Cross-Origin Resource Sharing) is a browser rule. It lets a server name which other origins are allowed to read its responses. The server names them in the `Access-Control-Allow-Origin` response header.

## What happens

1. Your React code calls `fetch("http://localhost:8080/api/users")`.
2. The browser sends the request. For some requests it first sends a preflight request. A preflight request is an `OPTIONS` request that asks the server whether the real request is allowed. The browser sends one when the request uses a method such as `PUT` or `DELETE`, a `Content-Type: application/json` header, or an `Authorization` header.
3. The API answers without `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: http://localhost:5173`.
4. The browser stops your JavaScript from reading the response and shows the CORS error in the console.

Often the server did receive the request and did answer it. The browser is the part that blocks it. That is why the same request works in `curl` or Postman: those tools do not apply CORS.

## Two fixes

**Option 1: Send the CORS headers from the API.** This is the fix to use when the frontend and API run on different origins in production too. Here is an example for Express:

```js
import cors from "cors";

app.use(cors({
  origin: "http://localhost:5173",
  credentials: true, // only if you send cookies
}));
```

For Spring Boot, add `@CrossOrigin(origins = "http://localhost:5173")` to the controller, or set up a global `CorsConfigurationSource`. Your server must also answer the `OPTIONS` preflight request with a 2xx status. If it returns 401 or 404 for `OPTIONS`, the browser blocks the request anyway.

**Option 2: Use the Vite dev proxy.** This is the fix to use when production serves the frontend and API from one origin. Vite forwards the requests, so the browser only ever talks to port 5173:

```js
// vite.config.js
export default {
  server: {
    proxy: {
      "/api": "http://localhost:8080",
    },
  },
};
```

Then call `fetch("/api/users")` with a relative URL, not `http://localhost:8080/api/users`.

| | API sends CORS headers | Vite dev proxy |
|---|---|---|
| Changes needed | Server code | `vite.config.js` and relative URLs in fetch calls |
| Works in production | Yes | No, the proxy only runs in the Vite dev server |
| Good fit | Frontend and API on different domains | Frontend and API behind one domain |

If you send me the exact console message and tell me which server framework you use, I can tell you which header is missing. Messages such as "No 'Access-Control-Allow-Origin' header" and "Response to preflight request doesn't pass" point to different causes.

`await` inside a `for` loop waits for each operation to finish before it starts the next one, so the wait times add up. `Promise.all` starts every operation first and then waits once, so the wait times overlap.

`await` pauses the `async` function until the Promise settles. In a loop, iteration 2 does not run until the Promise from iteration 1 settles. If each request takes 1 second, 3 requests take about 3 seconds.

`Promise.all` takes an array of Promises and returns one Promise. That Promise resolves when every Promise in the array resolves. When you write `urls.map(fetch)`, `map` calls `fetch` for every URL right away, so all requests are in flight together. The total time is about the time of the slowest request, which here is about 1 second.

```js
// Sequential: about 3 s for 3 requests of 1 s each
for (const url of urls) {
  const res = await fetch(url);   // the loop stops here until this request finishes
  results.push(await res.json());
}

// Concurrent: about 1 s
const results = await Promise.all(
  urls.map(async (url) => {
    const res = await fetch(url); // every request has already started
    return res.json();
  })
);
```

The speedup comes from overlapping the waits, not from running JavaScript in parallel. JavaScript still runs one task at a time. While a request waits on the network, the event loop runs other code, such as starting the next request. (The event loop is the mechanism that runs queued callbacks when the call stack is empty.)

| | `await` in `for` loop | `Promise.all` |
|---|---|---|
| When each operation starts | After the previous one finishes | All at once |
| Total time | Sum of all durations | Longest single duration |
| If one operation rejects | The loop stops there, and later operations never start | `Promise.all` rejects right away, but the other operations keep running |
| Order of results | Same as the input | Same as the input |

The sequential loop is still the right choice in two cases:

- **Each step depends on the previous result.** An example is paginated requests, where each response contains the cursor for the next page.
- **You need to limit concurrency.** An example is an API that allows 5 requests at a time. A helper such as `p-limit` or batching works here, because `Promise.all` alone would start all 1,000 requests together.

If you want every result even when some operations fail, use `Promise.allSettled`.

`useMemo`는 함수를 실행한 **결과 값**을 저장합니다. `useCallback`은 **함수 자체**를 저장합니다. 두 Hook 모두 의존성 배열의 값이 바뀌지 않으면 React가 이전 렌더링 때 저장한 것을 그대로 돌려줍니다.

여기서 memoization은 한 번 계산한 결과를 저장해 두었다가, 입력이 같으면 다시 계산하지 않고 저장한 결과를 돌려주는 기법입니다.

```jsx
function ProductList({ products, onSelect }) {
  // useMemo: 정렬 함수를 실행하고, 그 결과인 배열을 저장한다
  const sorted = useMemo(
    () => [...products].sort((a, b) => a.price - b.price),
    [products]
  );

  // useCallback: 함수를 실행하지 않고, 함수 자체를 저장한다
  const handleClick = useCallback(
    (id) => onSelect(id),
    [onSelect]
  );

  return <List items={sorted} onItemClick={handleClick} />;
}
```

`products`가 바뀌지 않으면 React는 정렬을 다시 실행하지 않고, 저장한 `sorted` 배열을 돌려줍니다. `onSelect`가 바뀌지 않으면 React는 렌더링할 때마다 같은 `handleClick` 함수를 돌려줍니다.

`useCallback(fn, deps)`는 `useMemo(() => fn, deps)`와 같은 동작을 합니다. 함수를 저장하는 경우가 많아서 React가 따로 이름을 붙인 Hook입니다.

| | `useMemo` | `useCallback` |
|---|---|---|
| 저장하는 것 | 함수를 실행한 결과 값 | 함수 자체 |
| 첫 번째 인자를 실행하는가 | 예, 렌더링 중에 실행 | 아니요 |
| 주로 쓰는 경우 | 계산 비용이 큰 값(정렬, 필터링)을 다시 계산하지 않을 때 | `React.memo`로 감싼 자식 컴포넌트에 함수를 props로 넘길 때 |

`useCallback`이 필요한 이유는 다음과 같습니다. 컴포넌트 안에서 선언한 함수는 렌더링할 때마다 새 함수 객체가 됩니다. 자식 컴포넌트를 `React.memo`로 감싸도 props로 받는 함수가 매번 달라지면, React는 props가 바뀌었다고 보고 자식 컴포넌트를 다시 렌더링합니다. `useCallback`으로 같은 함수를 넘기면 React가 자식 컴포넌트의 리렌더링을 건너뜁니다.

`React.memo`로 감싸지 않은 자식에게 함수를 넘기면 `useCallback`을 써도 리렌더링 횟수가 줄지 않습니다. 자식 컴포넌트는 부모가 렌더링될 때 항상 함께 다시 렌더링되기 때문입니다.

